{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,12,-6⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12y-6z-30 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-12y+30 \\\\ z & =2y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-5⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-5⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,12,-6⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a5208ba3a6d61c1d854213da0bd411d1d488a847f908e755c4b38c9fe7efa72b"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336241" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-1,1)$, $P(2,0,3)$ and $Q(1,1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-1,1⟩,\\,p=⟨2,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x-y-2z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a5208ba3a6d61c1d854213da0bd411d1d488a847f908e755c4b38c9fe7efa72b"} {"question" "q-33913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+3z-4=0"] ["math" "5x-y-2z-4=0"] ["math" "5x-y-2z=0"] ["math" "x-y+z+4=0"] ["math" "x-y-4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5x-y-2z-4=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨5,2,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,3,3⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-15j+15k \\\\  & =⟨3,-15,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z-48 & =0 \\\\ x-5y+5z-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a5208ba3a6d61c1d854213da0bd411d1d488a847f908e755c4b38c9fe7efa72b"} {"question" "q-33912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5x-2y+z+16=0"] ["math" "5x-2y+z=0"] ["math" "x+5y+5z+16=0"] ["math" "x-5y+5z-16=0"] ["math" "x-5y+5z=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-33912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-5y+5z-16=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-2,1)$, $P(5,0,3)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,11z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-2,1⟩,\\,p=⟨5,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,3⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-10i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨-10,4,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =⟨5,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17 \\\\ -10x+4y+11z & =-17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z & =17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z-17 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a5208ba3a6d61c1d854213da0bd411d1d488a847f908e755c4b38c9fe7efa72b"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336384" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "17"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-128462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-2,3)$, $P(3,-1,4)$ and $Q(-1,0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-128462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,3⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-1,4⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,4⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+2j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =⟨3,-1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z-10 & =0 \\\\ 4x-2y-6z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-128462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a5208ba3a6d61c1d854213da0bd411d1d488a847f908e755c4b38c9fe7efa72b"} {"question" "q-128462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-128462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-4y-6z+13=0"] ["math" "4x-2y+6z+21=0"] ["math" "4x-2y-6z+10=0"] ["math" "4x-6y-2z+17=0"] ["math" "4x-6y-2z+7=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-128462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-2y-6z+10=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,1,-1)$, $P(3,4,2)$ and $Q(-1,2,0).$\n\n$\\,y\\,-\\,z\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,p=⟨3,4,2⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,3⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =⟨3,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a5208ba3a6d61c1d854213da0bd411d1d488a847f908e755c4b38c9fe7efa72b"} {"question" "q-336381" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "q-336381" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/state.json" "file_sha256" "5e04658479f49bbd05387a621e48423098a3ef7c96d9cc4b97acaa9d66dbcee7"} {"question" "e-4936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4936.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3513ada3bef748824e0e9f4a7f141e3f67cdaab401ccada61f4e60a202d69"} {"question" "e-4936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4936.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3513ada3bef748824e0e9f4a7f141e3f67cdaab401ccada61f4e60a202d69"} {"question" "e-4937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4937.json" "file_sha256" "45aed9213052394e8e98892d1d1e4bc8dc8d0ee1f155b30363e95fd7a9b6912f"} {"question" "e-4937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4937.json" "file_sha256" "45aed9213052394e8e98892d1d1e4bc8dc8d0ee1f155b30363e95fd7a9b6912f"} {"question" "e-4938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4938.json" "file_sha256" "872f8b172637688bee49d5c145564650f68089d7422f4260e501425d94809a46"} {"question" "e-4938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4938.json" "file_sha256" "872f8b172637688bee49d5c145564650f68089d7422f4260e501425d94809a46"} {"question" "e-4939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4939.json" "file_sha256" "8a04740a5b6ffabf41d062547c296d3ee3b4bb4eb46036d78240e502d340aa90"} {"question" "e-4939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042721/example-4939.json" "file_sha256" "8a04740a5b6ffabf41d062547c296d3ee3b4bb4eb46036d78240e502d340aa90"}] "usage" {"e-4936" [] "q-33912" [] "e-4939" [] "q-33923" [] "e-4937" [] "e-4938" []} "basis" 1570 "retractions" [[17592186120217 :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $x - y + 4z + 16 = 0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r - p) \\cdot n = 0$."] [17592186120216 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1, 0, 3)$ and is parallel to the vectors $v_{1} = ⟨5, 2, 1⟩$ and $v_{2} = ⟨0, 3, 3⟩$."] [17592186119570 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}$."] [17592186119570 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= ⟨9,-2, 3⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z - 56 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119571 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}$."] [17592186119571 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFrom the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle$. So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= ⟨ - 1, 4, 8⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z - 33 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119572 :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi$, find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0$."] [17592186119572 :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y + z + 13 &= 0 \\\\\nz &= -2x + y - 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-13$. So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi$. Note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n(r - ⟨0, 0,-13⟩) \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 1⟩ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119573 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle$."] [17592186119573 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v_{1} \\cdot v_{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-16i - 4j - 14k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2,-6⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z - 124 &= 0 \\\\\n8x + 2y + 7z + 62 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117797 17592186117798 17592186117799 17592186117800 17592186117801 17592186117803 17592186117804 17592186117805 17592186117806 17592186117807] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117796 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117796 :answer-field/id] [17592186117796 :answer-field/key] [17592186117796 :answer-field/type] [17592186117802 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117802 :answer-field/id] [17592186117802 :answer-field/key] [17592186117802 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
